1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,872
How does ecom get a certificate from the card zone without handing over its secret key?

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:08,136
To get a passport, you send an application with your photo.

3
00:00:08,186 --> 00:00:10,704
The passport office checks it and stamps it.

4
00:00:10,754 --> 00:00:13,488
It never needs your face, only the photo.

5
00:00:13,538 --> 00:00:15,360
A caller does the same.

6
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:19,272
It makes its private key in its own Vault, where the key stays.

7
00:00:19,322 --> 00:00:23,664
It sends only an application: a certificate signing request.

8
00:00:23,714 --> 00:00:30,504
The card zone's certificate authority, its passport office, checks that the caller is on its list, and signs it for a limited time.

9
00:00:30,554 --> 00:00:41,376
One folder comes back: the certificate, the office's own seal so the caller can recognise the real card zone, a secret for the login server, and the addresses to call.

10
00:00:41,426 --> 00:00:43,920
The caller stores the folder in its own Vault.

11
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:47,496
No private key ever crosses between the two products.

12
00:00:47,546 --> 00:00:52,800
And every certificate is watched, so a warning goes out well before it expires.

13
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:57,168
Next: how the card zone stops a thief testing stolen cards.
